            <ArticleTitle>A systematic review of the effect of the distension medium on pain during outpatient hysteroscopy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>264-71</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.fertnstert.2010.04.080</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the effect of distension medium on pain during outpatient hysteroscopy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Systematic review and meta-analysis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Outpatient hysteroscopy clinics.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Women undergoing outpatient hysteroscopic examination.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Use of normal saline versus carbon dioxide as distension medium for outpatient hysteroscopy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Pain scores.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT(S)" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There was no statistically significant difference in pain scores when carbon dioxide or normal saline were used as the distension medium for outpatient hysteroscopy (standardized mean differences=-0.05; 95% CI, -0.17 to 0.07; I2=92%).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION(S)" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Carbon dioxide and normal saline are both suitable distending media for outpatient hysteroscopy as the procedural pain is comparable and the views obtained are satisfactory. However, normal saline does confer advantages that may make it more suitable for clinical use.</AbstractText>
